    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Performance Art"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1004>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/159>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/324> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Art in which the medium is the artist's own body and the work of art takes the form of actions performed by the artist."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/128> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/17> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bühnenräume"@de,
        "Stage spaces"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/728> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Stage space is the actual space on stage and the set in its multiple variations in the course of theatre history (permanent, changing, no set at all, realistic, suggestive, painted on wings, built etc.). It comprises also the trap doors, the revolving stage, the other technical equipment and lighting systems."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1449> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chalkoi"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1447>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bronze fractions of the obol. In Greece proper they were first introduced in Corinth at the end of the 5th c. BCE. According to ancient sources their value was equivalent to 1/8 of the obol or more often to 1/12 of it."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1453> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/22> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Countermarks (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2187> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Letters, inscriptions, numbers or other symbols punched into a coin at some point during its circulation. These marks were usually added to confirm the value of an old coin, accept its usage in a new area or change its face value."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1463> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1475> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Industrial archaeology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/118>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1678>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2470> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Interdisciplinary field aiming at understanding the industrial past, through systematic study of structures, artifacts and generally of technical monuments which are the material remains of the industrialisation of societies."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1498> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aureliani"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1496>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2484> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Roman silver-plated coins. They were first introduced during Aurelian's reform, in 274 CE, to replace antoniniani. Τhey were originally struck weighing 1/80th of a Roman libra and with the fineness in silver guaranteed to a constant 5%."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1517> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1513> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antonymy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Antonymy is the semantic relationship that exists between two (or more) words that have opposite meanings. The pairs of words which have opposite meanings are called antonyms. Antonymous pairs of words usually belong to the same grammatical category (i.e., both elements are nouns, or both are adjectives, or both are verbs, and so on)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1529> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1525> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Meronymy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Meronymy is a semantic relation specific to linguistics, distinct from the similar metonymy. A meronym denotes a constituent part of, or a member of something. That is, &quot;X&quot; is a meronym of &quot;Y&quot; if Xs are parts of Y(s), or."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1540> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1539> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Calvinists"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/458> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Christian Protestants who follow a set of teachings originating from John Calvin (16th century) and other theologians of the Reformation. Within this set, particular emphasis is given on the soteriological importance of predestination and divine grace, the power of sin over humanity, and the distinction between the elect and the non-elect. Calvinists, who differ from other Protestants in the interpretation of the eucharist and the total rejection of icons, follow several Churches, whose differences are often of historical nature."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1547> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Literary canon"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1860>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1900>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2538> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Literary canon has come to designate these literary works, that, by a cumulative consensus of critics, scholars, and teachers, have come to be widely recognized as &ldquo;major,&rdquo; and as literary classics. These literary works are the ones which, at a given time, are most likely to be included in anthologies and in the syllabi of college courses."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1564> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/575> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Eastern Orthodox Christians"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1589>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/458> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Christians who follow one of the Eastern Orthodox Churches, which constitute a communion of Patriarchates and autocephalous Churches that acknowledge the Patriarch of Constantinople as primus inter pares. Eastern orthodoxy emphasizes the unbroken continuity since the first Christian communities through apostolic succession, sacred tradition, dogmatic recognition of the seven ecumenical councils, organisation of the clergy, and religious practices. The secession between the Eastern Orthodox and Catholic Churches took place with the schism of 1054. The Eastern Orthodox Christians, who are particularly numerous in Eastern and Southeastern Europe, constitute the second largest Christian community in the world."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1589> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1655> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Patriarch"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1509>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1564>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1568> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bishop of a see with a special status within the historical development of Christianity, who is the head of an autocephalous ecclesiastical organisation (patriarchate). The patriarchal office, which goes back to the early Christian Churches (Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch, Jerusalem), is in use today in the Eastern and Oriental Orthodox Churches (mainly), a swell as in the Catholic Church."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1616> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Die studies"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2189> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Technical numismatic studies that involve the identification of the dies used to strike a coinage. Such studies allow for the establishment of the relative chronology of a coinage. They also permit quantification of a coinage by identifying the number of dies used to produce a given series."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1623> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Petrology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1610>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1641>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2548> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Subdiscipline of geology that studies rocks, including the processes of their production, their structure, mineral composition and classification, as well as their origin and history. Petrology was inseperable from mineralogy until the second half of the 19th century."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1636> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/22> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Majuscule script"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Script type according to which all the letters are contained between a pair of (real or theoretical) horizontal lines."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1650> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/702> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ηeadings of decrees (Epigraphy)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/573>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/742> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Structural elements of a typical Attika decree. They consist of the invocation of the gods, frequently inscribed with widely spaced letters at the top of the steale. The formula ἀγαθῆι τύχηι (\"by good fortune\") is common in the Roman imperial period. The headings may contain the decision or the name of the secretary or archon e.g. Μεθοναίον ἐκ Πιερίας (\"for the Methonians of Pieria\")"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1654> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Amendments of decrees"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/702> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Αdditions of decrees (Epigraphy)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/573>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/742> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Structural elements of the typical Attic resolution. These are the additions made by the Assembly to the text of the proposal to be voted on. In this way, the amendment was distinguished from the text of the proposal. Later, its text wasincorporated into the text of the proposal. After 275 BC. additions no longer appear in decrees."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1655> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1584> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bishop"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Ordained member of the Christian Churches who is elected as governor and overseer of an ecclesiastical district and of the respective community of the faithful. The office of the bishop originates in the reorganization of the early Christian Church by Paul, and is considered to continue the tradition of the Apostles (apostolic succession). Bishops have the right to ordain clergy and to convene in order to take decisions on important matters that concern the Church in its entirety."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1661> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Induction (Reasoning, Logic)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1575>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1581>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1662> ;
    skos:scopeNote "In the narrow sense, inference to a generalisation from its instances. In the broad sense, any \"ampliative inference\", i.e. any inference where the content of the conclusion goes beyond the content of its premises. Contrary to deduction, induction may have true premises and a false conclusion, which means that induction is \"not necessarily truth-preserving\"."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1662> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Deduction (Reasoning, Logic)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1575>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1581>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1661> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A finite sequence of sentences whose last sentence is a conclusion of the sequence and follows necessarily from the preceding sentences (premises), which means that if the preceding sentences are true then the conclusion must be true. Therefore, valid deduction is \"necessarily truth-preserving\", in contrast to induction. Furthermore, deduction is \"non-ampliative\", i.e. the conclusion bears no new content, since its content is already present, at least implicitly, in the premises."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1678> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/20> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Industrialisation"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1463>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2221>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2470> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Historical process which marks the transformation of a (local, regional, national, continental, or global) economy to an industrial one. The reallocation of capital, labour and other resources towards industry induces extensive and complex economic, social, cultural, political, geopolitical, as well as technological changes."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1721> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/26> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Prytaneuousae tribes (Ancient Greece)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1715>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1716>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1722> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A term that first appears in ancient Athens during the times of Athenian politician Cleisthenes. This term designates the tribes from which originated the fifty Council members who formed a prytany for a certain period of time."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1767> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1742> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Syngrapheis (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1082>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1777>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1778> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Committees appointed by the Assembly to prepare a financial and technical study (syngraphe, as it was called) for a public work."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1777> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/240> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Syngraphai (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1082>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1767>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1778> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Type of study for public works prepared by specialised committees (syngrapheis) in ancient Athens. This is the proposal of the syngrapheis, i.e. the general description of the public work, a financial and technical study which was usually related to parts of the construction. Construction projects could be assigned in sections."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1790> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/20> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Religious events"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Events that are not included in worship practices (e.g. prayer, liturgy, baptism), but refer to the development of religions and religious organisations (e.g. Churches) over time, and designate changes in the relations between them, as well as between them and other social, political, and economic agents."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1800> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tetartemoria (coins)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1447>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2351> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Silver coins minted in ancient Greece, valued at 1/4 of an obol."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1802> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/702> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Parabase"@de,
        "Parabassis"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1287>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1806>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/257> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Element of the attic comedy, irrelevant to the plot of the play, when the dramatic action is suspended, becoming a means of expressing the views of the poet. The Chorus addresses the audience directly breaking the dramatic illusion to announce the views, proclamations, and the advice of the playwright, stepping temporarily aside from its dramatic part. Usually parabassis followed the agon, while at the early stages of attic comedy it used to end the play."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1809> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Euthynai"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1808>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1821>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1822> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Euthyna was the examination of accounts which every public official underwent on the expiry of his office. In ancient Athens the examination consisted of two parts; the logos (account) concerned the handling of public money by the official and was the responsibility of a board of ten logistai (accountants); the euthynai proper was the process whereby any citizen could raise an objection as to the official's conduct in office, and was the responsibility of a board of ten euthynoi appointed by the council. These officials could dismiss the accusations or pass them on to the courts. Comparable accounting procedures are attested epigraphically in many other Greek states, oligarchic as well as democratic."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1812> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Quadrigati"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1491>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2351> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Roman silver didrachms of the Republican period, with an obverse type of a youthful, Janiform head and a reverse showing Jupiter in a quadriga driven by Victory. They were introduced after the First Punic War (264-241 BCE) and after several reductions in their purity, they were replaced in 211 BCE by the denarius."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1815> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pentanummi"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1504>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Copper Byzantine coins (5th-8th c. CE), which were first introduced by Anastasius in 498 CE, and were equal with 5 nummi."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1817> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Decanummi"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1504>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Copper coins which were issued by the Ostrogoths (6th c. CE), and were equal with ten nummi."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1827> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Semisses"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1492>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Small Roman bronze coins that were valued at half an assarius or six unciae."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1838> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1837> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pandemics"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2102>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2190>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2377> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Epidemics that expand over a very large geographical scale and even over the largest part of the planet, and which have important demographic, economic and social consequences on a global scale."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/186> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/26> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Religious communities"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/256> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of individuals who have the same faith, dogma and practices of worship, share a common system of cultural and metaphysical meanings, and develop a sense of common identity."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1862> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Graphe paranomon"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1069>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1956>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/69> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A legal procedure that occurs in ancient Athens. According to Athenian law, a graphe was a public criminal prosecution. Through the graphe paranomon any citizen could bring an accusation against the proponent of any law or decree which in his view was illegal and seek to have it abolished, at the same time indicating which law it had contravened. A law or decree was illegal if it had any substantial or formal defects, such as the omission of the probouleuma from the council."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1880> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Quadrantes"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1492>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Roman bronze coins equivalent to the 1/4 of an assarius. Τhey were first introduced as cast coins during the 3rd c. BCE and they bore three pellets as a mark of value. After ca. 90 BCE the quadrantes became the lowest-valued coins in circulation. They continued to be minted until the time of Antoninus Pius (138-161 CE)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1889> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Owls"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Glaukes (coins)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2360>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2366> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The ancient Athenian coins that bore the image of an owl on the reverse."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2090> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/21> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ephebeia (Αρχαία Ελλάδα)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1140>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2091>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2092> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An institution that appears in ancient Athens. It involved the \"basic military training\" of Athenian adolescents from their 18th to their 20th year. The training lasted two years, the first year it took place in the area of Piraeus, and in the second year, after the state provided the adolescents with arms and armour, they were sent to protect the borders of Attica."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2091> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kosmetes of the epheboi (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1140>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2090>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2092> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An office that appears in ancient Athens. The kosmetes was an elected official who headed the training of the Athenian ephebes (adolescents)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2092> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sophronestes (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1140>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2090>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2091> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An office that appears in ancient Athens. The sophronistai were ten elected officials (one from each tribe) who were responsible for the military training of the Athenian ephebes (adolescents)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2103> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1888> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Microscopes"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1602>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1603>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2377> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scientific instruments that utilize rays for the magnification of small objects, which may be viewed directly, photographed or recorded electronically. Microscopes were invented around the early seventeenth century and have played a crucial role for the advancement of biomedical and physical sciences."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2105> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1888> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Telescopes"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2195>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2432>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2611> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Instruments that collect electromagnetic radiation from distant objects, using mostly lenses, mirrors, or both, in order to produce an image of these objects or enable the radiation to be analysed.&nbsp;The first telescopes used for systematic observations of the sky in the early 17th century were constructed with lenses and played a significant role in the establishment of the heliocentric system and the new astronomical theories of the Scientific Revolution."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2155> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Trientes"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1492>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Roman bronze coins produced during the Roman Republic. They were valued at one-third of an assarius and were last issued around 86 BC."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2167> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/285> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Graphe"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "A legal procedure that occurs in ancient Athens. According to Athenian law, a graphe was a public criminal prosecution."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2177> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Theatrical properties"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Requisiten"@de,
        "Props"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2175>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2176>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/822> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Α prop is anything movable or portable on stage or screen used by actors during a performance or a screen production. In practical terms, properties is everything that is not part of the set, the costumes or the lighting."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2180> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1454> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Emergency hoards"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A hoard of coins, and perhaps other precious objects, secreted together in antiquity at a time of emergency. Such deposits tend to consist of a cross-section of coins in circulation at the time of deposit. They may thus serve as evidence for the nature of the coin supply at a given time."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2181> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1454> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Savings hoards"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of coins gathered together in a single deposit over a period of time for the purpose of saving."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2182> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1454> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ritual deposits (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of coins deposited together, not for the purpose of saving, but for religious purposes, such as offerings to gods. Unlike many hoards, such deposits were arguably not intended to be recovered."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2183> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Single finds (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Coins found in isolation, either by accident, or by deliberate means such as metal-detecting. This may or may not occur within a controlled archaeological survey or excavation environment."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2189> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/702> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Issues (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1616> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Specific subsections of a period of coin production, identifiable by specific control-marks or mint-magistrates’ signatures."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2211> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2004> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Die-engravers (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1465>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The artists responsible for the engraving of the design on to dies. They are generally anonymous on ancient coinage, although a few signatures are recorded, particularly in late fifth-century BCE Sicily."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/225> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/205> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Primary production"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The economic activities related to the extraction and/or production of natural products and raw materials."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2274> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/240> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Commedia dell'arte"@de,
        "Commedia dell'arte"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2279>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2281>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2298> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Form of improvised comedy that evolved in 16th-century Italy by professional actors, as opposed to the amateurs of the scholarly commedia erudita. Commedia&#39;s performances were based on skeletal scenarios, always very sketchy, that were not written in advance by a playwright, but were the collective creation of actors improvising words and gestures. Its main innovation was the employment of fixed types (tipi fissi), that were repeated from one play to another, with stereotypical features and behaviour, costumes and masks that covered half the face, leaving the mouth uncovered. Grouped in homogeneous troupes, the actors travelled throughout Europe and this theatre of the actor went on to enjoy success, becoming its main entertainment. This form of theatre had major influence on dramaturgy and theatre practice."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2323> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dokimastes"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2325> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Athenian official who sat near the banking tables in the agora of Athens and had the task of examining and approving Attic silver coinage. Counterfeits were slashed by the dokimastes, removed from circulation, and dedicated to the Mother of the Gods. There is evidence for the office of dokimastes at Athens as early as 398/7 BCE."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2325> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Counterfeits"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2323>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2484> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Privately manufactured coins of low intrinsic value that copy the appearance of official currency for the sake of deceitful profit."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2331> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/28> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scientific paradigm"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1457>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2332>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A body of laws, theories, concepts, applications, instrumentation, values, beliefs, methodological and metaphysical commitments, adopted by a group of scientists (scientific community) within the context of current scientific practice (\"normal science\"). In a second elaboration of the concept, made by its proponent, Thomas S. Kuhn, \"paradigm\" may be captured as \"exemplar\", referring to the problem solutions, accepted by the scientific community and used as models in the course of scientific training."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2332> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/20> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scientific revolutions"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2331>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2469>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Non-cumulative developmental episodes in which an older \"paradigm\" is replaced in whole or in part by a new one. The normal-scientific tradition that emerges from a scientific revolution is not only incompatible, but often actually incommensurable with that which has gone before, meaning without any common measure in the way scientists see the world and practice science in it."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2361> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Turtles (coins)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2360>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2363> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Coins of Aegina that bore the image of a turtle on their reverse."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2366> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1467> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Attic weight standard"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1889>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2365> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The system that was based on the silver tetradrachm of 17.2 gr. with subdivisions such as the drachm of 4.3 gr, the hemidrachm of 2.18 gr., the obol of 0.73 gr. etc. The attic weight standard originated from the Euboeic standard and at first included didrachms/staters of 8.6 gr."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2368> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1467> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Persian weight standard"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1829>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1851> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The weight standard that was introduced by Darius I, who at first followed Croesus’ standard, with a silver stater of 10.75 gr. and a gold stater of 8.06 gr. Later on, he adopted a weight of 8.36 gr. for the gold stater (dareikos) and 5.5 gr. for the silver stater (siglos)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2374> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Copper tetartera"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2489> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Copper Byzantine coins that were first introduced in 1092 CE by Alexios I Komnenos (1081-1118 CE) to replace the old follis. Their exact value is still a matter of controversy. From the reign of Andronicus II (1282-1328 CE) onwards they were replaced by a copper coin that came to be called an assarion, after the Roman coins."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2376> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Solidi stellati"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1507>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2353> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Gold Byzantine solidi of Constantine IX (1042-1055 CE), which had two stars on either side of the imperial bust on their reverse."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2421> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Moral philosophy"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1581> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ethics"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2459> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The branch of philosophy that focuses on the study of morality and can be divided into the general study of goodness, the general study of right action, applied ethics, metaethics, moral psychology, and the metaphysics of moral responsibility, although these divisions are not clear-cut. The first two (the general study of goodness and the general study of right action), constitute the main business of ethics and its principal substantive questions are what ends we ought, as fully rational human beings, to choose and pursue, and what moral principles should govern our choices and pursuits. How these questions are related is the principal structural question of ethics.<br />\"Ethics\" is also commonly used interchangeably with \"morality\" to mean the subject matter of this study; and sometimes it is used more narrowly to mean the moral principles of a particular tradition, group, or individual."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2428> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pentadrachms"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1446>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2351> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Silver quarters of a stater, issued by Philip II (360-336 BCE) and Alexander III (336-323 BCE). They weighed 2.15 gr. and were equal to five silver drachms of 4.30 gr., with a gold-silver ratio at 1:10."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2429> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Aspra trachea"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1504>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1820> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Byzantine, concave, billon coins, sometimes even made of pure copper. They were first issued by Alexios I Komnenos (1081-1118 CE) and were equal to 1/48 of the hyperperon or 100 nummi."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2443> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Meals in the prytaneion (privilege in ancient Athens)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2445> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A privilege granted to Athenian citizens in ancient Athens. Very rarely and in exceptional circumstances, the honorand was granted the right to free meals for life in the prytaneion, or even the right to free meals for his descendants. According to legislation, the right to free meals in perpetuity was granted as early as 5th century BC to generals who had won great victories. The grant of free meals for life was accompanied by front seats at the games and the erection of a statue. This grant was registered in the honorific decree which recorded the resolution of the Assembly."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2444> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Prohedria (privilege in ancient Athens)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2443>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2445> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A privilege granted to Athenian citizens and foreigners in ancient Athens. Prohedria was the right to watch the contests and plays from privileged seats, usually in the front row of the benches, along with the priests and city magistrates. It was commonly granted along with free meals in the prytaneion. The grant of this privilege was registered in the honorific decree which recorded the resolution of the Assembly."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2445> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erection of a statue (honor awarded)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2443>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2444> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An honor awarded to Athenian citizens and foreigners in ancient Athens. The pedestal of the statue was inscribed with a brief statement of the reasons for the award of the honor, such as the achievement of an important feat, or the benefactions on behalf of the city, or even the fine execution of the duties assigned to the honorand. During the Hellenistic period, the erection of a statue became commonplace, particularly when honors were being awarded to rulers."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2459> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Morality"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2421>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2425>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2462> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An informal public system applying to all rational persons, governing behavior that affects others, having the lessening of evil or harm as its goal, and including what are commonly known as the moral rules, moral ideals, and moral virtues."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2462> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2421> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moral psychology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2459>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2463>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2525> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Moral psychology is a field of both philosophy and psychology. On the one hand, it is the part of philosophy, where philosophy of mind and ethics overlap, which concerns all the psychological issues relevant to morality. On the other hand, it is the subfield of psychology that traces the development over time of moral reasoning and opinions in the lives of individuals."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2471> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/28> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technological determinism"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1824>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2221>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2470> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The idea which describes the relationship between technology and society in two related ways. The first is a theory of technology, arguing that the development of technology proceeds in an autonomous manner, and the design of technological artefacts and systems is determined by an internal, technical logic independent of social influence. This view has been criticised by the theory of social construction of technology. The second is a theory of society, arguing that technology is the cause or determinant of societal change and that, after all, technological change equals social progress."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2475> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/248> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technoscience"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2422>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2448>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The amalgamation of science and technology in an entire complex. According to actor-network theory of science and technology studies, technoscience includes both human (scientists, engineers, patrons etc) and non-human actors (laboratory equipment, artefacts, established knowledge, istitutions, money etc), which create larger and stronger networks, interacting in a social, economic and political level."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2481> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Stigeas"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Charakter (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1464>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1465> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The upper, moveable die of the coin which the minter struck on the hot flan during coin minting."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2483> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Anvil"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Akmoniskos (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1464>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1465> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The lower, fixed die of the coin, οn which the hot flan was placed during coin minting."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2484> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Subaerati"@en ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plated coins"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1498>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2325> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Coins, often counterfeits, made of copper, bronze, lead or iron and plated with a precious metal layer of silver, electrum or gold.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2488> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Half stavrata"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1819>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2351> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Large, heavy (4.40 gr.), silver coins of the late Byzantine Empire (14th-15th c. CE) which had half the value of the stavrata.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2489> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Half tetartera"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2352>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2374> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Copper Byzantine coins which had half the value of the copper tetartera. They were issued in Thessalonike and in some unknown mint in Greece during the 11th-13th c. CE.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2490> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Half tremisses"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2121>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2353> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Gold coins that were struck at 1/432 of the Roman libra. They were issued by Justin II (565-578 CE), Phocas (602-610 CE), Constantine IV (668-685 CE) and Philippicus (711-713 CE)."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2496> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Theoros (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2497>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2498>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2499> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An office that appears in ancient Greece. The theoroi were a city&#39;s representatives at another city&#39;s festival. At the festivals the theoroi offered sacrifices in the name of their cities. In other cases, they acted as envoys to receive an oracle from a sanctuary. From the 3rd century BC. theoroi made the announcements of the Panhellenic games to all Greek states."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2497> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Theorodokos (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2496>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2498>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2499> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An office that appears in ancient Greece. The theorodokoi were persons who welcomed and were responsible for the hospitality of the theoroi who arrived in their city. In addition to the persons, the term was also used for the cities that hosted the theoroi of other cities."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2498> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Theoriai"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2496>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2497>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2499> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An institution in ancient Greece that was associated with the representation of city-states in major events. The city-states sent delegations, consisting of theorists, who represented their city at major festivals or visited an oracle to receive an oracle. The expenses for conducting theoriai were paid either entirely by the state or by the architheoros or shared by the state and the architheoros."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2499> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1696> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Architheoros (city-state period)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2496>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2497>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2498> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An office that appears in ancient Greece. The architheoroi were the leaders of the theoriai. In some cases, the architheoroi undertook the costs of a theoria, either alone or jointly with the state.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2538> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1858> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Literary criticism (Discipline)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1334>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1547>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/215> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The discipline of describing, interpreting, and evaluating literary works, ιncluding varying methodological approaches to texts; the respective schools can be grouped according to text-, author-, reader and context-oriented approaches.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2558> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1659> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antiphrasis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Antiphrasis is a figure of speech in which a phrase&nbsp;or a word is employed in a way that is opposite to its literal meaning, in order to create an ironic, sarcastic or comic effect. Forms of antiphrasis are: litotes, irony and euphemism."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2581> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2253> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Academies"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Societies of learned individuals, with restricted membership, organised to advance arts, sciences, letters, music, or other cultural or intellectual areas of endeavour.&nbsp;The term refers not only to groups of learned persons but also to&nbsp;institutions of learning."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2600> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1888> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seismographs"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1832>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1836>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2520> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Instruments that record ground oscillations, which are caused, for example, by earthquakes, volcanic activity, and explosions. The first quite precise seismographs were constructed in the second half of the 19th century, contributing significantly to the shaping of the discipline of seismology.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2607> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2525> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Social psychology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/127>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1709>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/35> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The study of the nature, functions and phenomena of social behaviour and of the mental experience of individuals in social contexts. It includes the study of social effects on aspects of behaviour and mental experience that are studied more generally in other branches of psychology. It also includes a number of psychological phenomena that do not arise, or in some cases cannot even be delineated, in individuals outside of their social contexts, such as&nbsp;aggression and anger, altruism and helping behaviour, attraction and social relationships, cooperation and competition, sexual behaviour, social learning and socialisation etc.&nbsp;"@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2627> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2377> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Psychiatry"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2102>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2190>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2525> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A speciality of medicine concerned with the diagnosis, treatment and study of mental diseases or disorders. Psychiatry was formed as a self-conscious scientific project in the late 18th century, through the development of new techniques for treating &quot;madness&quot; and the making of a new institution, mental hospital or asylum, which was considered the appropriate setting and an active instrument for the cure of &quot;madness&quot;, until, by and large, the end of the 19th century."@en .

