<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/693> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/339> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unilineal descent"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/108>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/398>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/443> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Descent system whereby an individual&#39;s decent is traced exclusively through the maternal or paternal line of descent."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/731> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/559> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marriage"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/301>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/351>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/530>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/68>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/780> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Socially acknowledged conjugal act between two persons (spouses). As an institution, marriage constitutes a regulatory framework for the established family, and defines the spouses&#39; mutual obligations and rights towards their offspring, other persons within or beyond the family, and institutions."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/785> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/469> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Subordinate noun clauses"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Noun clauses are subordinate clauses that are used like nouns--as subjects, objects, and complements. Most languages have two types of clauses that function like nouns. One type is commonly found after verbs used to report knowledge: know, think, believe, feel, and others. Generally, the noun clause is the direct object of such a verb and is attached to the verb using that. The other type of noun clauses includes the wh-clauses as nouns-as subjects, objects, and complements of a main clause."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/83> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Parts of speech"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/138>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/330>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/88> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Parts of speech are a category of words which have similar grammatical properties. Words that are assigned to the same part of speech generally display similar behavior in terms of syntax—they play similar roles within the grammatical structure of sentences—and sometimes in terms of morphology, in that they undergo inflection for similar properties."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/849> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/586> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gebundenes Morphem"@de,
        "Bound morpheme"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1023> ;
    skos:scopeNote "A bound stem is a stem which cannot occur as a separate word apart from any other morpheme."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/852> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/417> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anachronies (Narratology-Methods)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Types of narration that consist of various forms of discordances between the two temporal orders of the succession of the events in the story and the pseudo-temporal order of their arrangement in the narrative."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/94> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Engravers' tools (Epigraphy)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Tools used in antiquity for the preparation of the surface intended to be inscribed, as well as for the engraving of the letters on it, for example, boasters and scapels."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/966> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/425> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antiken Theatern"@de,
        "Ancient theatres"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/146>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2172>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2173>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2174>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/87> ;
    skos:scopeNote "As an edifice, the ancient Greek theatre is an open-air amphitheatrical construction of a semicircular ground plan around a circular space. After the 5th century BCE and the flourishing of ancient (Greek) drama specific theatre buildings were built for performing religious ceremonies, staging artistic/theatre performances and hosting political events. Gradually the theatre acquired its form known from the archaeological findings, comprising three distinct parts: the koilon (for the audience), the orchestra, built as a full circle, and the scene <strong><em>(</em></strong><em>skênê</em><strong><em>),</em></strong> a low rectangular independent building situated behind the orchestra."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1025> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/594> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Modern Greek dialects"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The linguistic varieties of Modern Greek can be classified along two principal dimensions. First, there is a long tradition of sociolectal variation between the natural, popular spoken language on the one hand and archaizing, learned written forms on the other. Second, there is regional variation between dialects. The competition between the popular and the learned registers, culminated in the struggle between Dimotiki (Demotic Greek) and Katharevousa during the 19th and 20th centuries. As for regional dialects, variation within the bulk of dialects of present-day Greece is not particularly strong, except for a number of outlying, highly divergent dialects spoken by isolated communities."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/108> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/26> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kinship groups"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/101>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/339>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/342>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/443>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/693>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/940> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of people related by blood or affinity bonds."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/116> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Griechische Sprache"@de,
        "Greek language"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Greek language is an independent branch of the Indo-European family of languages, native to Greece, Cyprus and other parts of the Eastern Mediterranean and the Black Sea. It has the longest documented history of any living Indo-European language, spanning more than 3000 years of written records. Its writing system has been the Greek alphabet for the major part of its history; other systems, such as Linear B and the Cypriot syllabary, were used previously."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1296> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/933> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Computerlinguistik"@de,
        "Computational Linguistics"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Computational linguistics is an interdisciplinary field concerned with the statistical or rule-based modeling of natural language from a computational perspective. Traditionally, computational linguistics was usually performed by computer scientists who had specialized in the application of computers to the processing of a natural language. Computational linguists often work as members of interdisciplinary teams, including linguists, language experts (persons with some level of ability in the languages relevant to a given project), and computer scientists. In general, computational linguistics draws upon the involvement of linguists, computer scientists, experts in artificial intelligence, mathematicians, logicians, philosophers, cognitive scientists, cognitive psychologists, psycholinguists, anthropologists and neuroscientists, among others."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/138> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ancient Greek syntax"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/201>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/330>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/48>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/748>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/83>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/88> .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1454> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/24> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coin hoards"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2349> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of coins that have been withdrawn from circulation and deliberately brought together (but not necessarily deliberately placed in the ground). Hoards were traditionally divided into four basic categories: accidental losses, savings hoards (that sometimes represent many years of savings), emergency hoards (e.g. in the face of a military attack), and, finally, abandoned hoards (e.g. foundation hoards or burial hoards). This strict categorisation however, is now considered problematic by many scholars."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1456> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/29> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Field (Numismatics)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1471>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1472>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2187>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2188> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The usually empty space between the design and the edge of the coin. In this area subsidiary symbols such as mint-marks, control-marks or legends may be placed."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1457> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/208> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scientific communities"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1657>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1899>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2331>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2439>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2469>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups of scientists who work in the same research area and share the same fundamental perceptions of the nature and methodology of science."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1491> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Denarii"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1496>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1501>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1812>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2099>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2351> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Standard Roman silver coins that were introduced during the Second Punic War, in 211 BCE, to replace the quadrigati. They were originally equal to 10 asses, but around 141 CE they became equal to 16 asses."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1504> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nummi"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1815>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1817>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1818>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1820>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2429> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Argentiferous copper-alloy Roman coins that were first introduced in 294 CE with the monetary reform of Diocletian and weighed 1/32 of a libra. After 307 CE their weight suffered gradual reductions until 348 CE when they were replaced by maiorina."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1513> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/22> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Semantic relations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Semantic relationships are relations that describe hierarchically and taxonomically the links between two or more terms / words / lexemes and correspond to the horizontal or vertical axis of the semantic hierarchy."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/153> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arithmetic mean"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/160>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/174>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/599>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/619>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/641>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/86> .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1575> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1581> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Philosophy of science"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1508>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1661>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1662>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2469>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:scopeNote "The branch of philosophy that is centered on the critical examination of science, trying to understand its aims and methods, together with its principles, practices and achievements. One field of the philosophy of science, methodology, is closely related to the theory of knowledge, while other fields touch upon metaphysics and the philosophy of language."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1584> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1583> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Christian religious offices"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/458>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/575> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Religious offices in the framework of Christian communities and ecclesiastical organisations from the emergence of Christianity until the present. In the development of Christian religious offices are reflected the divergences and historical courses of the different Christian branches."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1641> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mineralogy"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1610>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1623>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2347>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2467>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2542>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2548> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Science which studies minerals, that is naturally occuring solids of usually inorganic substance, with a definite chemical composition and crystalline structure. It includes fields such as crystallography, crystalline chemistry and physical properties of minerals. From the 16th to the early 19th century, mineralogy was indivisible from petrology, and bridged chemistry and natural history. During the 19th century, it lost this commanding position with respect to the study of earth, and became a subdiscipline of geology, whereas in the second half of the 19th century it was seperated from petrology."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1651> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/702> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Prescripts of decrees (Epigraphy)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1218>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1716>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1720>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1953>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/573>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/742> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Essential structural parts of a typical Attika decree, in which the date elements of the decree are indicated; the name the eponymous archon, the name of the tribe that held the prytany, the name of the secretary of the Council, and the name of the chairman of the Council and Assembly."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/174> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geometric mean"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/153>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/160>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/279>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/515>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/694>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/86> .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1820> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1445> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hyperpyra"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1444>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1504>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1819>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2353>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2429>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2431> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Gold, concave Byzantine coins of 20.5 carats. They were first introduced in 1092 CE by Alexios I Komnenos and they were equal to 12 miliaresia or 6.000 nummi. They ceased to be minted during the 14th-15th c. CE and the name remained in use thereafter solely as a money of account."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1858> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1801> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Literary studies"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1860>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1900> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Discipline dealing with the study of the written or oral verbal works of a national or language community deemed to have an aesthetic value, often due to deploying language in ways that differ from ordinary usage."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1860> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/240> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Literature (body of works)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1547>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1858>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1900>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2205> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Literature<strong> </strong>is any body of written or oral verbal works of a national or language community deemed to have an aesthetic value, often due to deploying language in ways that differ from ordinary usage. The term is used since the eighteenth century to designate fictional and imaginative writings. In Romance languages the term &quot;literature&quot; designates all types of collections of written work (including philosophy, history, or scientific works addressed to a general audience)&nbsp;that are especially distinguished in form and expression. In Greek the term applies more narrowly to written or oral verbal works of an aesthetic value."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1989> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/21> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Migration"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2033> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Movement and establishment of people within a relatively long range of geographical distance and for a relatively long period of time. Relative spatial and temporal range depends on whether the individuals depart from a familiar cultural context and are introduced into another, and on whether the duration of their establishment is enough for the completion of a series of economic and social processes."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/201> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nominal"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/138>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/330>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/748>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/88> .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2082> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/21> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Proxeniai (Ancient Greece)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1407>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2080>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2081>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2083>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2087>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2198> ;
    skos:scopeNote "An institution that appears in ancient Athens and is related to the offer of hospitality and of private and public services to Athenian citizens in other cities of Greece by their citizens. The institution existed since the Archaic period and was of a private nature, as it conserned the relationship between kings and nobles. With the establishment of democracy, the proxenia came under the jurisdiction of the Athenian Demos."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2083> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/25> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Proxenia (honor awarded)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1407>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2080>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2081>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2082>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2087>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2198> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Αn honor awarded to foreigners in antiquity in Athens for the services they offered to Athenian citizens who visited the city of honored persons. The honor was accompanied by a series of privileges that were valid in Athens, as well as in the areas of its influence."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2097> a skos:Concept ;
