<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1093> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/584> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Catalogues (Public inscriptions)"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2193>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/573> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Inscriptions containing lists of persons and things that were either part of the structure of other inscriptions or were themselves self-contained inscriptions that were the subject of public dedication, either votive or honorary. Their subject matter varies according to needs, for instance lists of adolescents, proxenoi (consuls), ships etc."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/17> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://vocabs.dariah.eu/bbt/Concept/000020> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Structural parts of material objects"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This class classifies objects especially constructed to be parts of a complex material object. These objects have autonomy in relation to the complex object of the appropriate type, to which they are intended to be added. Despite their autonomy, however, they are not independent in terms of their intended function, but are structural parts of the object, ie they have a specific function within the module to which they belong and which they form.<br />Note: The structural parts of the material things are not considered narrower terms of the aggregation to which they belong."@en ;
    skos:topConceptOf <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1837> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/14> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Epidemics"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2102>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2190>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2377> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Temporary but widespread severe outbreaks of an infectious disease which affect a large part of a population and may result in sudden spikes of mortality, economic drawback, demographic change and social upheaval. Epidemics are due to diverse factors (ecological, genetic, social, etc.) and vary according to their extent and ferocity."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2190> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2002> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Health professionals"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1837>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1838>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2102>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2377>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2423>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2627> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Health professionals conduct research; improve or develop concepts, theories and operational methods; and apply scientific knowledge relating to medicine, nursing, dentistry, veterinary medicine, pharmacy, and promotion of health. Tasks performed by health professionals usually include: conducting research and obtaining scientific knowledge through the study of human and animal disorders and illnesses and ways of treating them; advising on or applying preventive and curative measures, or promoting health; preparing scientific papers and reports. Supervision of other workers may be included."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2223> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ecology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1462>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1538>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1602>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1603>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2347>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2362>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2426>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2458>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2548> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Science that studies the structure and functioning of nature as a system, and more specifically the interrelationships between organisms and their natural environment, both living and nonliving. Organisms are studied in the context of the populations and communities in which they can be grouped and the ecosystems of which they form a part. Moreover, the study of ecological interactions provides important information on the nature and mechanisms of evolutionary change."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2253> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broadMatch <https://vocabs.dariah.eu/bbt/Concept/000007> ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/26> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Organisations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This term classifies social collective entities that are structured and managed to meet specific needs or to pursue collective goals. The adoption of common beliefs, goals and objectives plus the plans to achieve them on behalf of a number of individuals is what permits classifying this type of social collective entity into organisations. Organisations typically stem from some relevant founding event, registered and documented or incorporated within specific narratives of origin, whereby the social collective entity they define acquires its identity and legitimisation. As a result, they exhibit organisational properties, which in their turn can change over time in order to adapt to new requirements."@en .

<https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2362> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2529> ;
    skos:inScheme <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zoology"@en ;
    skos:related <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1462>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1538>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/1602>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2223>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2347>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2434>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2457>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2458>,
        <https://humanitiesthesaurus.academyofathens.gr/dyas-resource/Concept/2522> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Science that studies animals, including their anatomy, physiology, biochemistry, genetics, ecology, evolution and behaviour. The term &quot;zoology&quot; as the general science of animals emerged during the 18th century with the spliting of natural history into distinct branches. However, already from the 16th and 17th centuries, the study of animals and subfields like ornithology and entomology were the subject of independent studies."@en .

